Figuring out stuff for yourself in nethack is, on the one hand, pretty cool
since there's tons and tons of stuff you could find out. On the other hand,
figuring out stuff for yourself in nethack can be very difficult for the
same reason. Cause and effect in nethack are sometimes less then obvious.

A spoiler is a piece (or a whole collection) of information that tells you
something you could've found out yourself. It "spoils" the fun of the
discovery. Reading rgrn will spoil you sooner or later. If you avoid all
the YAAP, YASD posts and anything that has the word spoiler in it, it will
be later. If you want to be spoiled in a fun way, just read the YAAP and
YASD posts and see how people solved the host of problems they faced
(or didn't). The good thing about most of the ascension posts is, you can
stop reading them when you think the author is getting to a point that's
far beyond your current reach and see if whatever you learned from the
post(s) up till then helps you...

Example: If someone told you that 
"They say that a <certain monster> might bring you luck.", I wouldn't
consider that to be a spoiler. If someone told you what monster and how
that works and what the consequences of it are and such, that would be
a spoiler (it is, in fact, a spoiler. Available here:

http://www.steelypips.org/nethack

which contains most of the spoilers and links to other really useful sites).

If you want to know exactly how nethack does something, grab the source from
www.nethack.org and have a look yourself :).

When I started playing nethack, most of my activities was to see how nethack
would manage to kill my character this time. So I tried a lot of things and
even found out some stuff for myself. Since then, my characters survive a lot
longer (thanks mostly to rgrn) so I'm quite a bit more paranoid about doing
things that seem to be too risky and often consult the spoilers beforehand.

So if you want to get somewhere "on your own" in nethack I'd suggest trying 
to find a solution yourself and if that doesn't work, ask the newsgroup. If
you'd rather just have some hints and not the complete solution to your
problem, just say so when you ask...

Good luck with your further adventuring!
